A||||9||strdream||The real deal about drop spindles even for the '88s ||||1||
Z||000000||strdream||11-21-2000||07:56 PM||||OK I'm looking at making modifications to the drop spindles for the '84-87 to accommodate bigger wheel brgs and brakes. I think I can do that with a minimum of change in the casting pattern so the cost should not kill me. I will have to wait until after the foundry runs the next batch and than I can take the pattern back and modify it.<BR>Now what about the '88s. Did you ever wonder why some other companies that offered spindles for Fieros don't anymore? Well here's my take on it. Bell Tech is a good example of a fine company, offering good products at resonable prices. They have tremendous resouces and capabilities. It costs a lot to maintain these resources.  With set up costs and marketing, you have to see a significant volume of sales to support the project. Than you add Research & Development, well it's just hard to justify. Me, I'm just a little guy. You wouldn't believe how little I am.  But I don't have the overhead to support either.  I have fewer resources and I don't have the support of the high volume shop so it takes me longer as many of you have noticed. Persistence is my Game Plan.  I have been three years developing the ones I have now. The next step should be easyer and faster.<BR>Now about the '88s<BR>I have never even seen an '88 suspension. I have no idea if it is a little or a lot differant from the '87. If I could modify machineing or castings to make these fit?  Maybe some kind of adaptor?  I don't know but I am willing to look into it.  I can get the front suspension for an '88 deliverd here with control arms, brakes, rotors, calipers the works for $150. The problem is that I don't want to buy these things not knowing if I will ever need them. All I want is to look at them and get some measurements and determine if I think I can do it.  If so I would go into it with the idea of offering a brake upgrade right off the bat. This hindsight stuff hurts.<BR>This is where I need your help.  Would someone send me one side, (I don't care which side) what I call a loaded spindle. I will return it when I make a desicion. Or maybe if I figure I can do it I will buy it from you.<BR>E-mail  strdream@iserv.net <A HREF="http://www.iserv.net/~strdream" TARGET=_blank>www.iserv.net/~strdream</A> <BR>thanks<BR>Ross      ||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||11-22-2000||08:43 AM||||Ross,<P>The guy you need to talk with on this forum goes by the screen name "mrfiero".  His real name is Mike Valentine and he runs a fiero business out in Colorado specializing in 88 Fiero's.  I am sure that he will have what you are looking for.<P>Hey Mike,<P>Ross is a stand up guy.  If you aren't using a spindle for a while loan it to him.  Yes I saw your previous post to someone else with your only requirements of making you a set.  You will have to work that out with Ross though :~)<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000002||turbo86se||11-22-2000||10:18 AM||||If you can get an entire '88 setup for $150, get it!!  When you're done with the parts, you'll be able to get that back when you sell the parts. I'm looking to pay upwards of $300 for the entire '88 suspension, front and rear, from a wrecked '88 coupe, just to have them around for my '88 coupe, now almost outfitted with a V6!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Raydar||11-22-2000||11:13 AM||||Ross,<BR>I'm the person who e-mailed you regarding the 88. (Thanks for the quick response!) <BR>The 88s are <I>completely</I> different from the earlier years. The hubs are one piece sealed bearing/hub assemblies that are fastened to the upright (knuckle? can't really call it a spindle) with three torx bolts. If you are familiar with the rear hubs, the fronts look similar with the exception of the drive axle splines. (There aren't any. Or any place for them.) <BR>As for a brake upgrade... The brakes on the 88's were reworked with larger vented rotors, so it <I>may</I> not be an issue. (I'm sure others will let me know if I'm wrong.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR>If mrfiero can't send you a spindle, I probably can, but it's going to take a while. Have many things to do before I can start taking parts off my parts car. <BR>BTW... I'm with Turbo86. $150 for a complete 88 front suspension is a deal! You shouldn't have any trouble recovering your costs.<P>Raydar (Steve)||||reg||1
Z||000004||Will||11-22-2000||12:25 PM||||strdream:<P>     Buy the '88 Front end. If you don't want it anymore once you are done measuring it, I will buy it from you for a reasonable price and pay for shipping.<P>E-mail: wlucke@shentel.net||||reg||1
Z||000005||strdream||11-23-2000||07:16 PM||||So far I like the sound of that. A knukle with a bolt on spindle would be easyer to make.  All I need to know now is if the ball joints, brakes and steering arm can be done with the same casting as the '87s. ||||reg||1
Z||000006||mrfiero||11-23-2000||07:28 PM||||I just got around to reading this thread, so forgive me for not responding sooner.<P>I have no problem sending a front spindle/knuckle (or 2)....Just LMK where to send it/them.  I would love to have a set for my GT, so LMK if I can help.<P>That said, if you can get an entire front suspension for $150 then that would be the way to go.  You can certainly get your money back out of it when you're done and it will show you the HUGE differences there are from the '84-'87 Fieros.    ||||reg||10
Z||000007||Will||11-23-2000||11:33 PM||||I hate working on '88 front suspension. I like driving it, but I hate working on it. NONE of the parts are common to ANYTHING else.<BR>When you make these Knuckles, please make them fit a more commmonly available bearing cartridge!<BR>     I think it would be beneficial for you to see what the '88 suspension looks like. It is VERY different from the early suspension. The early suspension has a bolt in crossmember; the '88 suspension has an entire subframe. The '88 assmebly probably weighs twice what the early assembly weighs.<BR>     I am serious about buying that suspension from you once you are done with it. If you decide not to go that route, I would appreciate it very much if you would put me in contact witht he person selling it.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000008||Pontiaddict||11-25-2000||02:32 PM||||This may be a pain to do, but how about using 4th gen F-body hubs in the front. They are a bolt in style and have a common wheel bolt pattern. Then using 4x4 S-10 front hubs for the back since they have the same bolt pattern. Spindles could be made in the early and late styles, using the bolt in hubs. And you could use the F-body brakes. <P>This would take a lot of work, and I don't even know if it's possible, but it's just a thought.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000009||strdream||11-28-2000||09:11 AM||||Pontiaddict<P>It sounds like you have a lot of knowledge of GM products and parts.  That would be useful in doing something like this.  I see you are in Michigan.  Maybe we could brainstorm this togather.<P>Ross ||||reg||1
